    77	
    78	The dual-color lockup is **the standard mark on every surface, in-product
    79	nav and dashboard included.** There is no monochrome-vs-dual split and no
    80	brand-surfaces-only reservation: the mark rests dual-color everywhere — the
    81	product nav, the dashboard, the console shell, and buyer-facing product UI all
    82	carry the Sid-blue + Avira-rose split at rest. The mark may still *pass
    83	through* the aperture-close animation during the transient states above (the
    84	§9.2 keyframes still apply during a simulation run and the decision-commit
    85	beat); the animation note remains valid. `ink` monochrome is no longer a
    86	mark color state — it is reserved for the wordmark and structure (§2).
    87	
    88	**RATIONALE (founder, 2026-06-26, ticket #35):** the founder chose visual
    89	brand consistency over the brand-vs-product split. One mark, one color story,
    90	on every surface a user or buyer ever sees — the dual-color aperture is the
    91	identity, not a special-occasion variant. This deliberately overrides the
    92	prior "monotone in-product / dual-color brand-surfaces-only / never resting
    93	in the nav or dashboard" policy.
    94	*(Two-state consolidation + everywhere-standard dual-color: founder decision,
    95	2026-06-26.)*
    96	
    97	### 1.4 Clear space
    98	
    99	Clear space minimum equals **one blade-length** (= mark width / 4) on every
   100	side. In horizontal lockups, the wordmark sits inside this clear space — it is
   101	the only thing allowed there.
   102	
   103	### 1.5 Mark assets
   104	
   105	| File | Use |
   106	| --- | --- |
   107	| `frontend/public/brand/sidira-mark-duo.svg` | **standard mark** — dual-color (Sid-blue + Avira-rose), used everywhere (§1.3) |
   108	| `frontend/public/brand/sidira-mark.svg` | legacy monochrome mark, `currentColor` stroke — fallback only, no longer default (§1.3) |
   109	| `frontend/app/icon.svg` | favicon / app-icon (duo-color mark on paper ground, `rx: 32`) |
   110	
   111	---
   112	
   113	## 2. The wordmark
   114	
   115	**`SIDIRA`** — narrow uppercase, no glyph trickery, no dots, no embellishments.
   116	
   117	| Property | Production target | MVP / fallback (shipping now) |
   118	| --- | --- | --- |
   119	| Typeface | **Söhne Schmal Halbfett** (Klim Type Foundry) | Archivo Narrow SemiBold (free, Google Fonts) |
   120	| Weight | 600 | 600 |
   121	| Case | uppercase always | uppercase always |
   122	| Tracking | `+60` (= `letter-spacing: 0.06em`) at 24 px and above | same |
   123	| Tracking (small) | `+80` (= `letter-spacing: 0.08em`) at ≤ 16 px | same |
   124	| Color | `ink` (`#0E1A2B`) default · `paper` on dark surfaces | same |
   125	
   126	The wordmark is never lowercase, never italic, never given a dot embellishment,
   127	never paired with a tagline inside the lockup. The lockup is mark + `SIDIRA` —
   128	nothing else.
   129	
   130	---
   131	
   132	## 3. Lockups
   133	
   134	Four sanctioned lockups. The primary horizontal is the one used by default,
   135	and it carries the **dual-color mark everywhere** (§1.3).
   136	
   137	| Lockup | Composition | Use | Asset |
   138	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
   139	| Primary horizontal | dual-color mark + `SIDIRA` left-aligned | nav · dashboard · email signature · press · default everywhere | `sidira-lockup-duo.svg` |
   140	| Stacked | dual-color mark above `SIDIRA` centered | marketing footer · business card · press kit | (compose at usage site — same proportions) |
   141	| Mark only | dual-color mark, no wordmark | app icon · favicon · social avatar · loading state | `sidira-mark-duo.svg` · `app/icon.svg` |
   142	
   143	The legacy monochrome lockup (`sidira-lockup.svg`) and mono mark
   144	(`sidira-mark.svg`) remain in the repo as fallbacks but are no longer the
   145	default on any surface (§1.3, ticket #35).
   146	
   147	### 3.1 Lockup proportions
   148	
   149	| Property | Value |
   150	| --- | --- |
   151	| Mark height | wordmark cap-height × **1.25** |
   152	| Mark-to-wordmark gap | mark width × **0.28** |
   153	| Mark vertical alignment | cap-height baseline matched to mark optical center |
   154	
   155	The provided `sidira-lockup.svg` and `sidira-lockup-duo.svg` files already
   156	encode these proportions. Use them directly rather than recomposing.
   157	
   158	---
   159	
   160	## 4. Color
   161	
   162	### 4.1 Architecture — three layers, three meanings
   163	
   164	| Layer | Token | Hex | Carries |
   165	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
   166	| **Structure** = Sid | `ink` | `#0E1A2B` | the entire ink layer — wordmark, body text, rules, structural foundation. Already navy-leaning. Sid is the thing everything else stands on. (The mark itself is no longer an `ink` monochrome surface — it rests in the standard dual-color lockup, `DUO_SID` + `DUO_AVIRA`, on every surface; §1.3.) |
   167	| **Signal** = brand-functional | `signal` | `#C8FF3D` | live state · the instrument is reading · oscilloscope-phosphor accent. No personal meaning. Countable budget: lime lives on **at most three carriers per screen** — a glass well's live traces (the well counts as one carrier; §9.1 sanctions two traces inside it), the §9.5 live carrier, and the active state of one control. Never on two adjacent elements, never as fill, never as background. |
   168	| **Vital** = Avira | `avira` | `#D696C8` | secondary accent for narrowly-scoped narrative surfaces — see §4.4. |
   169	
   170	**Lime's primary home is glass.** On glass (`#0A1421`) signal lime reaches
   171	~15.7:1 contrast and reads as phosphor — that is where it lives. On paper, lime
   172	requires an ink companion stroke at ≥3:1 contrast (this codifies the existing
   173	underdraw practice as law; bare lime on cream fails at ~1:1).
   174	
   175	**Phosphor persistence:** lime traces on glass get a 5 px / 18%-opacity
   176	underdraw of the same path. Never CSS glow, blur, or `drop-shadow`.
   177	
   178	### 4.2 Surface and text tokens
   179	
   180	| Token | Hex / value | Use |
   181	| --- | --- | --- |
   182	| `paper` | `#F4EFE6` | primary surface — archival-tungsten warm off-white |
   183	| `paper-elevated` | `#FAF6EE` | cards · modals · elevated panels |
   184	| `paper-recessed` | `#EBE5D8` | inputs · code blocks · receded panels |
   185	| `rule` | `rgba(14,26,43,0.16)` | hairline dividers, **`0.75px` CSS** — a true hairline (replaces solid `#D6CFC0`; unit law in §7.2 — never `pt`) |
   186	| `ink` | `#0E1A2B` | primary text · lines · wordmark (the mark rests dual-color — `DUO_SID` + `DUO_AVIRA`, §1.3 — not `ink`) |
   187	| `ink-muted` | `#4A5668` | secondary text · axis labels · captions |
   188	
   189	#### Glass — the instrument display
   190	
   191	| Token | Value | Use |
   192	| --- | --- | --- |
   193	| `glass` | `#0A1421` | the dark display surface — **live computed readings only** |
   194	| `glass-rule` | `rgba(244,239,230,0.08)` | hairlines on glass |
   195	| `glass-text` | `#F4EFE6` | primary text on glass |
   196	| `glass-muted` | `rgba(244,239,230,0.55)` | secondary text on glass |
   197	
   198	Glass is a **component surface, not a theme**. It is sanctioned ONLY for live
   199	instrument readings: confidence traces, CI axes, running states, live
   200	telemetry readouts. Never for chrome, navigation, tables, text content, cards,
   201	or marketing. If glass spreads, the brand bet dissolves into a generic dark
   202	dashboard — this rule is load-bearing. Glass wells take radius 4 and a 1 px
   203	`glass-rule` border.
   204	
   205	#### Layering
   206	
   207	- Recessed: `inset 0 1px 2px rgba(14,26,43,0.07)`
   208	- Elevated: hairline border + `inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,0.55)` top highlight
   209	- Drop shadows remain banned.
   210	- Maximum **3 paper levels + glass** on any screen.
   211	
   212	### 4.3 Semantic tokens
   213	
   214	| Token | Hex | Use |
   215	| --- | --- | --- |
   216	| `alert` | `#E84A2C` | error · weakness-flagged |
   217	| `warn` | `#D9A300` | underpowered · caution |
   218	| `success` | `#2F6B3F` | confirmed · ship-candidate affordances · positive deltas |
   219	| `signal` | `#C8FF3D` | live · settled · evidence (see §4.1) |
   220	
   221	`success` exists so that no surface ever invents its own green — it closes the
   222	root-cause gap behind the off-token green/blue/red hexes that accumulated in
   223	production. Token sweep happens in the rollout's PR 2.
   224	
   225	**Semantic colors are chip and accent colors, not prose colors.** Prose and
   226	body text are always `ink` or `ink-muted`. What each token may touch:
   227	
   228	| Token | Text? | Accent (borders · bands · markers)? |
   229	| --- | --- | --- |
   230	| `warn` | **never** — ≈2.0:1 on paper, fails WCAG at any size (Codex catch, PR #174 — now law). Mid-range score text falls back to `ink-muted` | yes — this is its only job |
   231	| `alert` | short mono designators and chips only, always co-signaled by a border or the wording itself (≈3.4:1 — below AA for small text; see §7.8). Never body text | yes |
   232	| `success` | short mono designators and chips (≈5.6:1 — AA) | yes |
   233	| `signal` | never text on paper (§4.1 — needs the ink companion); on glass per §4.1 | per §4.1 only |
   234	
   235	### 4.4 Sid + Avira — sanctioned use
   236	
   237	The personal-meaning colors appear in two color states of the mark (§1.3) and
   238	in five documented surfaces. Anywhere else is wrong.
   239	
   240	| Surface | Sid (`#2C5577`) | Avira (`#D696C8`) |

